ible rewrite per sweep.\" So we apply each pass in order; for each pass, we find the first occurrence of \"old\" in the current text and replace it with \"new\". If a pass does not find an occurrence, it does nothing. Then we record the passes that actually changed the artifact (the text) in this sweep. Then we repeat sweeps until no pass changes anything or max_rounds is reached. Then we return the final text, the list of application names that changed (in order? The pass schedule order? The output 'applications' is array of strings. The description: \"Record only passes that change the artifact.\" So we should record in the order they were applied? The spec says \"Apply passes in their registered order.\" So we should record the names of passes that changed the text in the order they are applied. But note: In each sweep, we apply each pass sequentially, and record if it changed. But if a pass does not change, we don't record it. Then we move to next pass. At the end of sweep, if any pass changed, we continue to next sweep (up to max_rounds). If none changed, we stop. Also need to check fixed_point flag: true if we stopped because no changes (i.e., fixed point reached) or false if we hit max_rounds? The description: \"Repeat full sweeps until none changes or the round budget is consumed. Return the artifact, application trace, and fixed-point flag.\" So fixed_point = True if we stopped because no changes (i.e., reached fixed point), else false if we stopped because max_rounds reached? It says \"or the round budget is consumed\" meaning we stop when either condition met. The output \"fixed_point\" boolean: likely true if we reached fixed point (i.e., no changes) before consuming all rounds, false if we exhausted rounds. However, if we exhaust rounds without reaching fixed point, then fixed_point=false. But note: If at the last sweep we still have changes, but round budget is consumed, we might have done the last sweep and then stop. In that case, after that sweep, we might have changes (since we applied passes) but then we check if budget consumed? Need to be precise: The logic: for round in range(max_rounds): perform a sweep: apply passes in order, record changes. If no changes happen in that sweep, break (fixed point reached). Else continue to next round. After the loop, if we broke because no changes, fixed_point=True; if loop ended because range exhausted (max_rounds rounds done), fixed_point=False. But careful: What if in the last round there were changes? Then after that round, we exit loop because max_rounds reached. The fixed point is not reached. So fixed_point=False. The output \"applications\" should contain the names of passes that changed in each sweep? The description: \"Record only passes that change the artifact.\" It says \"application trace\" - likely the list of all applications (pass names) that changed in order across all sweeps. So we should accumulate a list of pass names whenever a pass changes text. That list should be in the order of application across sweeps (i.e., sweep1: passes that changed in order, sweep2: passes that changed in order, etc.). That matches \"Record only passes that change the artifact.\" So we output that list as array of strings.\n\nEdge cases: Replacements are simple string replacements: replace first occurrence of \"old\" with \"new\". But note: \"Each pass performs at most its first eligible rewrite per sweep.\" So only the first occurrence? The description says \"at most its first eligible rewrite per sweep.\" So if there are multiple occurrences of \"old\", only the first one is replaced per sweep. That's typical for such pass scheduling. Also, after replacement, the text changes and subsequent passes in the same sweep see the updated text. And in next sweep, we start from the beginning of passes.\n\nAlso note: In the given fixture, passes are: halve-a: old \"aa\" new \"a\"; drop-b: old \"bb\" new \"b\"; drop-c: old \" cc\" new \"\". For initial text \"aaaa bb cc\". Let's simulate: Sweep1: pass halve-a: first occurrence of \"aa\"? In \"aaaa\", the first two characters are \"aa\". Replace with \"a\" => becomes \"aa bb cc\". (changed, record \"halve-a\"). Now pass drop-b: old \"bb\" exists? In current \"aa bb cc\", there is \" bb\" but old is \"bb\"? Actually old is \"bb\", but we need exact match. In \"aa bb cc\", there is substring \"bb\" (preceded by space). So replace first \"bb\" with \"b\" => \"aa b cc\". (changed, record \"drop-b\"). Pass drop-c: old \" cc\" (with space). In \"aa b cc\", there is \" cc\" at end. Replace with \"\" -> \"aa b\". (changed, record \"drop-c\"). So sweep1 changed all three. After sweep1: text = \"aa b\". Sweep2: pass halve-a: old \"aa\"? In \"aa b\", first two chars \"aa\", replace with \"a\" => \"a b\". (changed). pass drop-b: old \"bb\"? In \"a b\", no \"bb\". So no change. pass drop-c: old \" cc\"? No \" cc\" either (only \"a b\"). So sweep2 only halve-a changed. Sweep3: pass halve-a: old \"aa\"? In \"a b\", no \"aa\". So no change. No changes -> fixed point reached after sweep3? But max_rounds=8, so we break and fixed_point=True. The applications list: [\"halve-a\", \"drop-b\", \"drop-c\", \"halve-a\"]? But we need to check: In sweep2 only halve-a changed, so it's added. So final list: [\"halve-a\", \"drop-b\", \"drop-c\", \"halve-a\"]. Then fixed_point=True, text=\"a b\". But wait: Is that correct? Let's simulate more: After sweep2, text = \"a b\". Then sweep3: no changes, so break. So applications list includes passes from sweep1 and sweep2. That seems plausible.\n\nBut note: The output schema demands \"applications\" array of strings, \"fixed_point\" boolean, \"text\" string. So we need to compute that.\n\nImplementation: def solve(case): text = case[\"text\"]; passes = case[\"passes\"]; max_rounds = case[\"max_rounds\"]. Ensure they exist and are valid. Then iterate rounds. For each round, track changed_flag = False. For each pass in passes: find first occurrence of old in text; if found, replace